(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-practicum").sets.find(s => s.id === "13");
  const prose = [
  "Start by identifying what the system must protect, such as private records, account credentials, or the ability to provide service. Then consider who could misuse it and which paths would let them reach those assets. For example, a record-viewing endpoint needs protection against a user requesting someone else's record. Threat modeling turns that possibility into a design question about access checks. It is a structured way to choose defenses, not an exercise in listing every imaginable attack.",
  "Authentication establishes which identity a request can be associated with. Checking a password or validating a token is part of that process. After a successful check, the system may know that the caller is a particular account, but it still does not know that the account may perform every operation. A signed-in student should not automatically be able to change another student's grades. That second decision belongs to authorization and must be checked separately.",
  "Authorization determines whether an identified requester is allowed to perform a particular action on a particular resource. An instructor may be allowed to edit grades for one course but only view another course's public description. Merely knowing that the account belongs to an instructor is not always enough. The decision must apply the relevant rules to the requested action and object. This is why logging in successfully does not grant unrestricted access throughout an application.",
  "Suppose a reporting service only needs to read enrollment records. Giving it permission to delete accounts creates risk without helping its task. Least privilege limits the service to the access it actually needs. If its credentials are stolen or its code has a defect, the unnecessary deletion capability is not available to make the damage worse. The permissions still need to be sufficient for legitimate work and revisited when the service's responsibilities change.",
  "Hiding an edit button in the browser can improve the interface, but it does not protect the server endpoint. A caller can send a request without using that button or even that browser. The server must therefore check whether the requester may perform the operation before applying it. Client-side checks can provide fast feedback, while server-side enforcement supplies the actual protection. Treat the browser as a source of requests, not as an authority on its own permissions.",
  "Defense in depth uses controls that address different failure possibilities. A protected service might authenticate requests, enforce object permissions, restrict its database account, and record suspicious activity. If one control fails, another may still limit the consequences. Repeating the same flawed check in two places is weaker than using controls with different assumptions. The layers should have clear purposes and work together; adding more security features without understanding their coverage can create complexity without useful protection.",
  "Security requirements can be lost between design, implementation, and operation unless they are checked throughout the work. A design may require per-record permissions, while one new endpoint accidentally omits the check. Review, tests, and operational monitoring provide different opportunities to detect such gaps. Revisit the requirement when features or dependencies change. A security review performed only before the first release cannot establish that later versions still preserve the same protections.",
  "Think of authentication as establishing who is making the request and authorization as deciding whether that identity may carry it out. A valid login for Alex does not establish permission to read Jordan's private record. The application must use the authenticated identity together with the resource and action to make that decision. Keeping the two concepts separate prevents a common mistake: treating the existence of a valid session as sufficient permission for any requested operation.",
  "Every protected server action needs an effective permission check for the actual resource involved. Checking access on the page that displays a link is not enough if the underlying endpoint can be called directly. For example, a record download must verify that the requester may read that record before returning it. Consistent enforcement can be centralized, but it must still cover every relevant path. One unprotected alternate endpoint can undermine otherwise careful interface restrictions.",
  "An account should receive the permissions required by its job, rather than broad access granted for convenience. A backup reader, for example, may need to read selected data without changing application settings. Narrow permissions reduce what a mistaken action or compromised account can do. They are not a guarantee against all harm, because even necessary access can be misused. Review permissions as responsibilities change so old privileges do not accumulate indefinitely.",
  "Before choosing security controls, identify what would be valuable to an attacker and which adversaries are plausible in the system's setting. A public registration service might need to protect personal data and prevent automated abuse that blocks legitimate users. Those concerns lead to different design and testing questions. Threat modeling makes the assumptions explicit so the team can prioritize realistic risks. It should evolve with the system rather than remain a one-time diagram nobody consults.",
  "A trust boundary marks a change in what the receiving code can safely assume about incoming data or authority. A server receiving browser input crosses such a boundary because the caller can alter the request. The server must validate the data and enforce relevant permissions before treating it as legitimate. Boundaries also exist between services or privilege levels. Being inside the same organization does not automatically make every input trustworthy for every purpose.",
  "Suppose a bug allows an attacker to reach an unexpected server operation. If the server's database account is also unrestricted, the consequences may be much worse than necessary. A separate database permission limit can reduce the damage even though it did not prevent the original bug. That illustrates independent layers of defense. Each layer should cover a meaningful failure mode, and the team should examine common assumptions that could make several layers fail together.",
  "Anything delivered to a user's browser can be inspected by that user, including bundled JavaScript and its configuration. A private service credential embedded there should therefore be treated as exposed. Keep privileged operations and secrets on a trusted server, and let the browser request only operations the user is authorized to perform. Some public identifiers are intentionally shipped to clients; the important distinction is whether possession grants authority that the user should not have.",
  "Input validation checks whether data has the form and meaning the operation can safely accept. A purchase endpoint might require a positive integer quantity within a defined limit. Rejecting malformed or out-of-range values prevents those cases from reaching logic that assumes valid data. Validation is one control among several: it does not replace authorization or safe database parameter handling. Apply it where untrusted data enters and make the allowed conditions match the actual business rule.",
  "Testing only permitted actions can miss broken access control. A security test should also use an account that lacks permission and verify that the protected operation is denied without exposing data or changing state. For example, one student's account should not download another student's private record by changing an identifier. This checks the server's enforcement directly. A hidden button is not adequate evidence, because the test must cover requests that bypass ordinary navigation.",
  "An application relies on code it did not write, such as parsers, frameworks, and client libraries. A flaw in one of those dependencies can expose the application even when its own use of the library appears correct. Track relevant dependencies and assess published fixes in the application's context. Updating requires compatibility checks, but ignoring dependency risk leaves part of the executed system unexamined. Security reasoning must include the code that actually runs, not only code owned by the team.",
  "A risk deserves attention based on both how plausibly it can be exploited and what harm exploitation could cause. An exposed flaw that reveals private records may demand urgent work, while a difficult-to-reach issue with minor consequences may receive a different priority. These judgments depend on the system's context and available evidence. A score can help organize discussion, but it should not hide uncertainty or replace examination of who could be affected and how.",
  "A design change can invalidate an earlier security assumption. An internal-only endpoint may become public, a new role may gain access, or a dependency may introduce a different trust boundary. Revisit security requirements when those conditions change so protections remain appropriate. The original review was evidence about an earlier system, not a permanent certificate. Trace requirements into the changed design and tests to see whether the new behavior still enforces them.",
  "The check asks whether this user may read this particular record before returning protected data. That makes it authorization rather than merely authentication. Its effectiveness depends on a trustworthy user identity and a correct can_read implementation. All paths that expose the record must enforce the rule, and denial must happen before disclosure. The example shows the order of the decision clearly, but the surrounding system still determines whether the permission check has the information and coverage it needs."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-practicum/13");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-practicum").sets.find(s => s.id === "14");
  const prose = [
  "Software decisions can affect users, bystanders, support staff, and people whose data the system holds, even when they are not the paying client. A client may request a shortcut that exposes those people to avoidable harm. Professional responsibility requires considering those effects rather than treating payment as the only source of obligation. Understand who will be affected, communicate significant risks, and use appropriate professional channels when the requested work conflicts with responsible practice.",
  "A conflict of interest arises when another interest could interfere with independent judgment. For example, recommending a supplier while receiving an undisclosed benefit from that supplier gives others reason to question the recommendation. The technical option might still be good, but the decision process needs transparency and appropriate safeguards. Disclosing the interest allows the organization to decide how the recommendation should be evaluated. Concealing it prevents others from assessing the advice fairly.",
  "An estimate is a forecast based on incomplete information, not a guarantee created by choosing a precise number. If a task depends on an unfamiliar integration, a range and stated assumptions better represent what is known. For example, the estimate may be two to four weeks if access arrives on time. As evidence improves, revise it. Hiding uncertainty can make planning look simpler initially while leaving stakeholders unprepared when a known risk becomes real.",
  "A report should distinguish what was observed from what is inferred. \"The load test failed at 500 users\" is an observation; \"the database is the cause\" may still be a hypothesis. Presenting the hypothesis as settled fact can send the team toward the wrong fix. State the evidence, interpretation, and remaining uncertainty clearly. This is not reluctance to make judgments; it allows others to assess those judgments and choose the next investigation responsibly.",
  "Finding a credible safety concern creates a responsibility to communicate it to people who can assess and address it. For example, a test showing an unsafe control response should not be hidden because a release deadline is close. Preserve the evidence and use the appropriate reporting or escalation process. The concern may require further investigation, so communicate what is known and uncertain. Silence deprives decision-makers of information needed to protect affected people.",
  "A stakeholder cannot make an informed decision if a technical tradeoff is described only in jargon. Explain the concrete consequence: a cheaper storage option might make recovery slower, or a shortened test period might leave a particular failure unexamined. Include the benefit as well as the cost and state uncertainties. Clear communication helps people choose knowingly. It is part of engineering work because the decision often depends on consequences that extend beyond the implementation team.",
  "A software feature can change the situation of people who never approved or paid for it. For example, collecting additional user data may help a client while increasing exposure for the people represented in that data. Professional judgment considers those downstream effects alongside the client's goals. Identify foreseeable harms and communicate them in concrete terms. The immediate customer relationship matters, but it does not make everyone else's interests irrelevant to a responsible technical decision.",
  "Disclose a relevant conflict before others rely on your recommendation. If you have a financial interest in a vendor, the team should know that while comparing options rather than after signing a contract. Disclosure allows independent evaluation or reassignment where needed. It does not automatically settle every conflict, but it prevents the decision from resting on a false impression of independence. The relevant question is whether the interest could reasonably affect judgment about this recommendation.",
  "Saying a project will take exactly seventeen days can imply more certainty than the evidence supports. If key requirements or dependencies remain unresolved, express a range and explain the assumptions behind it. This gives planners a way to respond to uncertainty instead of discovering it through missed commitments. Update the forecast when those assumptions change. Precision is useful when justified; otherwise it can make a weak estimate look authoritative without making it more accurate.",
  "A status report should say what is complete, what evidence supports that claim, and what is expected next. \"The integration test passed\" differs from \"we expect integration to finish tomorrow.\" Mixing those statements can make predicted work appear accomplished. Label forecasts and their assumptions so stakeholders can plan appropriately. Honest reporting may include uncertainty or bad news, but it gives the team a usable picture of reality rather than a reassuring narrative that later decisions cannot rely on.",
  "A safety concern should reach someone with responsibility and authority to act on it. Record the observed behavior, conditions, potential consequences, and unresolved questions, then use the appropriate escalation channel. If the first response does not address a serious concern, follow the applicable process for further escalation. The aim is to get the risk assessed and handled, not merely to send a message that can later be cited as proof of having mentioned it.",
  "Claiming expertise you do not have can cause others to rely on decisions you are not equipped to evaluate. If a task requires specialized safety or security knowledge, state your limits and seek qualified review or assistance. This does not mean avoiding unfamiliar work altogether; learning with suitable support is normal. The professional obligation is to represent competence honestly and arrange the oversight needed for the consequences of the work.",
  "In a technical review, a senior person's confidence does not make a claim correct. If two reviewers disagree, examine the requirement, evidence, and consequences of each proposed choice. A small experiment or concrete example may resolve the issue more effectively than an argument about experience. Respectful disagreement can improve the result when it stays focused on the work. Personal status and attacks distract from the reasoning needed to decide whether the design is sound.",
  "When a deadline will be missed, report that promptly with the known cause, current progress, and a revised forecast. If a vendor delay is responsible, explain which work is blocked and what alternatives exist. Avoid hiding the problem until the original date passes, because others may still be planning around it. The new forecast should reflect remaining uncertainty rather than simply replace one unsupported promise with another. Useful reporting helps the team decide what to change next.",
  "Access to confidential data for one task does not authorize every other use. A developer who can inspect production records to diagnose a defect should not reuse them in a public demonstration or unrelated analysis without appropriate authorization. Keep the use tied to its permitted purpose and handle the data through approved processes. This distinction protects the people and organizations represented in the records. Technical ability to retrieve information is different from permission to use or disclose it.",
  "A decision can be convenient for the development team while shifting risk to users or operators. For example, skipping a data-migration check may save an afternoon but leave customers facing corrupted records. Consider foreseeable consequences for those downstream people when evaluating the shortcut. Identify who bears the cost if an assumption fails and communicate that clearly. Ethical reasoning becomes more useful when it examines the actual people and failure scenario rather than relying on abstract claims about doing good.",
  "A recommendation is easier to evaluate when its assumptions and tradeoffs are visible. If you recommend a hosted service because it reduces operational work, also identify dependencies such as availability, cost, and data-handling requirements. Explain which conditions would make another option preferable. This lets stakeholders assess the advice in their context and revisit it when circumstances change. Concealing the costs may make the recommendation sound stronger, but it makes the resulting decision less informed.",
  "A review sometimes needs to challenge a shortcut that makes delivery easier but leaves a significant risk uncontrolled. For example, bypassing a failed migration check may meet a release date while endangering stored data. Explain the failure scenario and evidence, then propose a safer path or appropriate escalation. The objection should be specific enough to evaluate. Professional responsibility includes raising such concerns even when agreement would be more convenient in the moment.",
  "If a test failed, report that it failed and describe the conditions and observed result. The team may later discover that the test was wrong, but that possibility does not justify calling the run a success before investigation. Distinguish the failed observation from a conclusion about its cause. Accurate reporting preserves the evidence needed for the next decision. Rewording inconvenient results to sound positive can lead others to release or rely on software without the confidence they think they have.",
  "The estimate depends on vendor API approval arriving this week. Stating that assumption explains why the two-to-four-week range might change even if the team's own work proceeds as expected. Stakeholders can track the approval and plan a response if it slips. The range still needs a basis in the work involved; adding an assumption does not make an arbitrary estimate credible. It makes one important source of uncertainty visible so the forecast can be interpreted and revised responsibly."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-practicum/14");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-practicum").sets.find(s => s.id === "16");
  const prose = [
  "An abstraction presents the behavior a caller needs while keeping unnecessary machinery out of the caller's way. A file interface lets an application read bytes without managing disk sectors. This reduces how much the application must understand to do its job. The abstraction still needs a clear contract, including relevant failures and effects. Hiding a detail is useful when callers can safely ignore it, not when they must discover it through surprising behavior.",
  "A data structure makes some operations easier than others. An array provides direct access by index, but inserting near its beginning may require moving many elements. A different structure may avoid those moves while making indexed access slower. Choose by the operations the program performs and their frequency. There is no universally fastest structure independent of the workload, and memory use or ordering requirements can matter as much as a single operation's running time.",
  "Concurrent tasks make progress during overlapping periods. A server might wait for one request's database response while processing another request. On one processor, execution can be interleaved rather than simultaneous; multiple processors may allow actual parallel execution. The distinction matters because concurrency introduces coordination questions even without parallel hardware. Tasks can still interact through shared state or depend on the order in which events occur, so overlapping progress does not automatically mean independent behavior.",
  "Suppose two tasks both read a counter as 10, each adds one, and each writes 11. One increment is lost because the read-and-update sequence was not coordinated. A different execution order could produce 12. The result depends on timing, which makes this a race condition. Protect the whole operation that must behave as a unit, not just one individual read or write. Tests may miss the race when their scheduling happens to avoid the harmful order.",
  "Linear growth means that the amount of counted work increases in proportion to input size, apart from fixed overhead and constant factors. A loop that performs one constant-cost check on each of n records does roughly n checks. Doubling the records therefore roughly doubles that work. This is a statement about growth, not a promise of an exact wall-clock time. The cost of each iteration and the computing environment still affect actual performance.",
  "A cache keeps a value so repeated reads can avoid an expensive calculation or retrieval. If the underlying value changes, the saved copy may become stale. For example, a cached product price can disagree with the current database price unless the cache is refreshed or invalidated appropriately. The speed benefit therefore comes with a freshness decision. Choose the policy according to what the application can tolerate rather than assuming that any cached answer remains correct indefinitely.",
  "The loop visits each item once and adds its cost to the total. If reading item.cost and performing the addition each take constant time, n items require work proportional to n. There is no nested scan or repeated search in the shown code. That is the basis for calling the loop linear. If item.cost secretly performs expensive work, the overall analysis must include that cost rather than infer complexity solely from the loop's appearance.",
  "Start with the questions the workload asks most often. If a program repeatedly finds records by a unique identifier, a map may fit well. If it needs to process records in sorted order, another structure or additional indexing may be useful. Consider updates, memory, and ordering requirements too. Selecting a structure by its familiar name or one favorable operation can create poor performance elsewhere when the real workload uses it differently.",
  "A race condition is a correctness problem caused by an uncontrolled ordering of operations. Two tasks reserving the final seat might both observe it as available before either marks it taken. Each check is individually plausible, but their combined execution violates the intended rule. Make the check-and-reserve operation atomic using an appropriate coordination mechanism. Merely adding delays or relying on one task usually finishing first does not remove the dependency on timing.",
  "Once a value is cached, the application must decide when it can no longer be trusted. An account setting changed in the database may remain old in a cache until expiration or explicit invalidation. That delay can be acceptable for some data and unacceptable for permissions or other sensitive decisions. Define the freshness requirement first, then choose a policy that meets it. A high cache-hit rate is not a success if it repeatedly supplies obsolete answers where correctness requires current data.",
  "A caller using a queue should need to understand enqueue and dequeue behavior, not the particular array or linked structure underneath. The abstraction boundary separates those concerns so the implementation can change without rewriting callers. Some details, such as whether operations can block or fail, may still matter and belong in the contract. A good boundary hides irrelevant mechanisms while exposing the information needed to use the component correctly.",
  "A linear scan examines items one after another. Searching an unsorted list may require checking every item when the target is absent or last, so the worst-case work grows with the list's length. It may stop sooner for a target near the front. State which case is being analyzed rather than confusing a lucky early match with a general guarantee. Repeated scans can also become costly when a larger algorithm performs them many times.",
  "A hash map uses a key to choose where to look for its associated value. That often avoids scanning every stored entry, which makes it useful for repeated identifier lookups. Its performance depends on factors such as hashing, collisions, and resizing; expected constant-time lookup is not an unconditional guarantee for every implementation and input. It also does not ordinarily maintain sorted key order. Match the structure to both the lookup needs and any other required operations.",
  "A lock can make a critical section exclusive so conflicting tasks do not update shared state at the same time. For a balance update, the protected section may need to include reading the old balance, calculating the new one, and writing it. Protecting only the final assignment can still permit lost updates. All relevant code must follow the same locking rule. Locks solve a coordination problem, but poorly chosen scope or ordering can create contention or deadlock.",
  "Suppose task A holds lock X and waits for Y, while task B holds Y and waits for X. Neither can continue to the point where it releases the lock the other needs. That circular waiting is a deadlock. A consistent acquisition order can prevent this particular pattern: both tasks would request X before Y. The important reasoning is about resources already held and resources being awaited, not simply whether the program uses more than one lock.",
  "To make a complexity claim meaningful, say what input size means and which operations are counted. A search may perform O(n) comparisons, while each comparison itself could involve a long string. Counting comparisons alone does not capture every character examined. State the cost model and include expensive operations when relevant. This makes the analysis useful for comparing algorithms and prevents a simple-looking loop from hiding work that changes the overall growth rate.",
  "A cache hit occurs when the requested value is already stored in a usable form. The program can return that value instead of recomputing it or fetching it from a slower source. A miss requires the underlying work and often adds the result to the cache. The benefit depends on how often values are reused and how costly retrieval is. The cached value must also satisfy the application's freshness rules; a fast obsolete answer may still be wrong.",
  "An immutable value cannot be changed after construction, so readers do not need to coordinate competing modifications to that value. Several threads can therefore share a properly published immutable configuration more easily than a mutable object with changing fields. This does not remove every concurrency concern: references may be replaced, surrounding state may change, and construction or publication must still be safe. Immutability reduces one source of interference rather than making the entire program automatically race free.",
  "A queue returns the item that has waited longest: the first one added is the first one removed. This models work such as processing incoming jobs in arrival order. If A, B, and C are enqueued, ordinary dequeue operations return A, then B, then C. A priority queue follows a different rule based on priority, despite sharing the word queue. Choosing the correct discipline matters because it changes which work receives attention next.",
  "A stack removes the most recently added item first. If A, B, and C are pushed, popping returns C before B and A. This fits nested work where the latest unfinished task must be handled before returning to an earlier one, such as function calls or undo history. The ordering rule is the defining feature, not the physical storage used. An array or a linked structure can both implement stack behavior."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-practicum/16");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-practicum").sets.find(s => s.id === "17");
  const prose = [
  "Induction proves a family of claims by establishing a starting case and a rule that carries truth forward. To prove a formula for every positive integer, verify it for 1, then show that if it holds for an arbitrary k, it also holds for k+1. The step alone is insufficient because it provides no starting truth. The base alone covers only one case. Together they justify every value reachable from that starting point.",
  "A universal claim says something holds for every allowed case. One valid case where it fails is enough to disprove it. If someone claims all prime numbers are odd, the prime number 2 is a counterexample. Check that the example satisfies the claim's assumptions before using it. A value outside the allowed domain does not refute the statement. Finding many supporting examples is different: they can suggest truth but do not prove a universal claim.",
  "An equivalence relation groups objects that are considered the same for a particular purpose. Equality of remainders modulo 3 is an example: each integer matches itself, matching works in either direction, and two matches can be chained. Those are reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity. They make the grouping consistent. A relation such as \"is within one of\" is not transitive, so it does not produce the same kind of well-defined equivalence classes.",
  "Probability assigns an event a value from 0 to 1 within a specified model. A fair six-sided die has probability 1/6 of showing a particular face because the model treats its six outcomes equally. The number depends on those assumptions; a biased die needs a different distribution. Probability quantifies uncertainty rather than predicting which outcome must occur on the next trial. Repeated observations can help assess whether the chosen model describes the situation adequately.",
  "A graph uses vertices for objects and edges for selected relationships between them. In a course-prerequisite graph, vertices represent courses and directed edges can indicate that one must precede another. This representation makes questions such as reachability or circular prerequisites easier to state. The graph does not automatically include every detail of the real situation. Choose what vertices and edges mean carefully, because the conclusions are only about the relationships the model actually represents.",
  "A loop invariant is a statement that remains true at a chosen point on every iteration. When summing an array, one useful invariant is that the accumulated total equals the sum of the items already processed. Establish it before the loop and show that processing the next item preserves it. At termination, all items have been processed, so the invariant explains the final total. A true but irrelevant statement would not provide that correctness argument.",
  "The set seen begins empty and gains at most one distinct value for each processed item. It therefore cannot contain more elements than the full input list, which explains the assertion. The property holds initially and is preserved by adding the next item. It is a valid invariant, but a weak one: it does not by itself prove that seen contains exactly the items processed so far. For that stronger conclusion, the proof needs a more informative statement about the set's contents.",
  "To refute \"every sorted list has distinct elements,\" the sorted list [1, 1] is enough. It satisfies the premise of being sorted and violates the claimed conclusion of distinctness. The validity of both parts matters. An unsorted list with duplicates would not be a counterexample to that statement. This method is powerful because a universal assertion promises all permitted cases; one genuine exception breaks that promise regardless of how many other examples support it.",
  "The base case starts the chain of an induction argument. If the claim concerns all integers n at least 0, verify the statement at 0 or otherwise cover the smallest allowed cases. Without a valid starting point, the inductive step only says that truth would propagate if it existed. Some recurrences require several base cases because the step depends on more than one earlier value. Choose enough starting cases to support every use of the step.",
  "The inductive hypothesis grants the claim for the smaller case or cases allowed by the proof. You then use that assumption to establish the next case without assuming the very conclusion being proved. For a recursive algorithm, this often means assuming recursive calls solve smaller inputs correctly and showing the parent combines their answers correctly. The reduction in size is essential. Calling the same-size problem and claiming the hypothesis applies would make the reasoning circular.",
  "An invariant must already hold when the loop reaches its first iteration. For a running sum, initializing total to zero establishes that it equals the sum of the empty processed prefix. If total starts at an unrelated value, the update rule might preserve a different, incorrect relationship forever. This is why initialization is a separate proof obligation. Preservation cannot repair a false starting assertion merely by being repeated many times.",
  "An invariant explains what remains true while the loop runs; the stopping condition explains what is additionally true when it ends. For a search, the invariant may say every earlier item has been checked. When the index reaches the list length, that covers all items. Combining those facts can establish the returned result. You also need a termination argument to know the loop reaches that point rather than continuing indefinitely while preserving the invariant.",
  "An equivalence class contains all elements related to a particular element under an equivalence relation. For integers grouped by remainder modulo 3, the classes are the three possible remainders. Every integer belongs to one class, and different classes do not overlap. Reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity make this partition possible. If a supposed grouping lets an element belong inconsistently to two distinct classes, check whether the underlying relation really has those properties.",
  "A directed edge distinguishes where a relationship starts and where it leads. If an edge A to B means A must be completed before B, reversing it changes the prerequisite rule. That direction affects reachability and possible orders. An undirected edge would instead represent a symmetric relationship without that distinction. When building or reading a graph model, state the meaning of the arrow before applying an algorithm; a correct algorithm cannot rescue a reversed interpretation of the edges.",
  "In an ordinary finite model where every possible elementary outcome has positive probability, an event with probability zero contains no possible outcomes. For a fair die, rolling a seven is such an event. The qualification matters: zero probability is not generally identical to logical impossibility in every probability model, especially continuous ones. Read the claim in its stated finite-model context and be explicit about which outcomes the model treats as possible.",
  "The intersection of two sets contains exactly the elements that belong to both. If one set is students taking algorithms and another is students taking maintenance, their intersection is the students taking both courses. Membership in either set alone is insufficient. This differs from union, which includes students in at least one of the courses. A concrete membership check helps keep the two operations distinct when translating a requirement into a set expression.",
  "A proof by contradiction temporarily assumes that the claim is false and follows that assumption to an impossibility. For example, a smallest-counterexample argument may show that an alleged smallest failing instance would imply an even smaller failing instance. That contradicts its choice as smallest. The contradiction must follow from valid deductions and the stated assumptions. Merely finding the opposite claim surprising or inconvenient is not a mathematical contradiction.",
  "A path follows edges from one vertex to the next, so consecutive steps must actually connect. In a directed graph, each step must also respect the edge's direction. A list of vertices with no connecting edge between two neighbors is not a path just because both vertices occur in the graph. Paths express reachability and can model routes or dependency chains. Definitions may restrict repeated vertices for a simple path, so use the terminology specified by the problem.",
  "A probability calculation depends on how outcomes are distributed and related. Saying two coin tosses each have a one-half chance of heads does not alone establish independence; that is an additional assumption needed for common multiplication rules. State the model before calculating. If the real process has bias or dependence, the answer from a fair independent model may be misleading. The arithmetic can be correct while the modeling assumptions are wrong for the situation.",
  "A termination argument identifies progress that cannot continue forever. A loop that decreases a nonnegative integer on every iteration must eventually stop, provided the decrease is strict and the loop cannot reset it upward. The bound prevents endless descent, while the decrease shows real progress. A variable changing somehow is not enough; it could cycle. For recursive code, the analogous argument shows each call moves toward a base case under a well-founded size measure."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-practicum/17");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-practicum").sets.find(s => s.id === "18");
  const prose = [
  "Engineering design searches for a solution that meets needs within real limits. A device may need accurate sensing, low power use, and a fixed cost, with improvements in one making another harder. Compare alternatives against those requirements and constraints rather than optimizing a single feature in isolation. Document the accepted tradeoffs so the choice can be evaluated. A design is successful in the context of its intended use, not because it maximizes every desirable property simultaneously.",
  "A model deliberately leaves things out so a question becomes manageable. To estimate traffic capacity, a model may represent average request arrivals and processing time without describing every interface pixel. That simplification is useful if the omitted details do not determine the answer. State the assumptions and limits, then compare the model with relevant evidence. A detailed model is not automatically better; unnecessary detail can obscure the relationship being investigated without improving the conclusion.",
  "A prototype can test whether a risky idea is workable before the team builds everything around it. If a design depends on a sensor responding quickly enough, a small experiment with that sensor can measure the delay. The prototype should target the uncertainty rather than imitate the finished product superficially. A successful demonstration answers only the question it actually tested. It does not establish production reliability, scalability, or maintainability unless those were part of the experiment.",
  "Encapsulation keeps state together with the operations that control its valid changes. An account object might allow withdrawals through a method that checks the balance instead of letting every caller overwrite the balance field directly. This creates one place to enforce the rule. Encapsulation is not merely putting variables inside a class. If callers can still mutate the state arbitrarily or depend on its representation, the intended protection is weak.",
  "Decomposition divides a system into parts small enough to reason about, with clear ways for those parts to cooperate. A registration application might separate enrollment rules from notification delivery. Their interface states what information passes between them and what each promises. Poorly chosen boundaries can create more coordination work than they remove. Evaluate the division by whether responsibilities and dependencies become clearer, not simply by the number of components created.",
  "A risk assessment considers both whether an adverse event is likely and how serious its consequences would be. A frequent minor display glitch and a rare loss of all customer data need different discussions. Estimate likelihood and impact using available evidence, and state uncertainty where it is substantial. Combining them helps prioritize attention, but a single score can hide important differences. The team still needs to understand the actual failure scenario and possible responses.",
  "A design choice rests on assumptions about its environment and use. Choosing local storage may be sensible when one person uses one device, but that assumption changes when the product must synchronize across many devices. Revisit the decision instead of defending it only because it once worked. The original reasoning helps identify what changed and which alternatives deserve consideration. Revisiting does not automatically mean replacing the design; it means evaluating it under the new conditions.",
  "Suppose the team wants to estimate whether a service can handle peak requests. A model may represent arrival rates, processing capacity, and queues while omitting interface styling. Those omissions keep attention on the capacity question. If the model also ignores a database bottleneck that dominates processing time, however, its simplification becomes misleading. The useful test is whether retained details support the intended question and whether omitted details could materially change the answer.",
  "An untested assumption can become expensive once many components depend on it. A prototype reduces that uncertainty by trying the critical behavior early. For example, a small integration can reveal whether a vendor API supports the required operation and latency. Record what was tested and what remains unknown. The prototype is evidence about a focused risk, not a promise that the final system will meet every requirement merely because the experiment succeeded.",
  "Breaking a system into components helps only when their responsibilities and interactions are understandable. A billing component might own fee calculation while a payment component handles a payment provider. Their interface should define the request, result, and relevant failures. Without that contract, decomposition can scatter one tangled problem across several files or services. Use the boundaries to reduce what each part must know, while preserving a clear account of how the complete behavior is achieved.",
  "If any caller can directly overwrite an object's fields, the object cannot reliably maintain rules about their relationship. An interval object, for example, may require its end to be no earlier than its start. Controlled operations can validate changes before accepting them. Encapsulation restricts direct access so those rules have a consistent enforcement point. The interface must still provide the legitimate operations callers need, or they will be tempted to bypass the boundary.",
  "Likelihood describes how plausibly a failure might occur; impact describes what happens if it does. Both matter when choosing where to spend engineering effort. A rare but catastrophic data-loss scenario may warrant protection even when a common inconvenience receives a lower priority. Estimates should include their assumptions and evidence. Treating uncertain judgments as precise measurements can make a risk table look more objective than it is and hide the need for further investigation.",
  "Suppose a system was designed around the assumption that a dataset fits in memory. As usage grows, that assumption may become false, changing the cost and feasibility of the original approach. Reconsider the decision using the new workload and constraints. This is easier if the original rationale was recorded. The goal is not constant redesign; it is to notice when the reasoning supporting a decision no longer describes the system that actually exists.",
  "A tradeoff means an improvement in one objective comes with a cost in another. Compressing data may reduce storage and transfer size while increasing processing time. Whether that is worthwhile depends on the system's constraints and workload. Make both sides visible and compare alternatives under realistic conditions. Calling a design \"better\" without naming the objective can conceal the cost that another user, component, or operator will have to bear.",
  "A simulation runs a model of a system to explore how it might behave. A queueing simulation can estimate waiting times under different arrival rates before the full service exists. Its conclusions depend on assumptions such as processing times and arrival patterns. Compare important predictions with real measurements when possible. Simulation can reveal likely bottlenecks and guide design choices, but it does not eliminate the need to validate whether the model represents the situation accurately.",
  "A requirement rules out solutions that do not satisfy it. If a device must run for a specified duration on a battery, designs that exceed its energy budget are unacceptable even if they perform other tasks well. Multiple requirements narrow the possible choices together. Some may conflict or prove infeasible, which calls for an explicit stakeholder decision. Treating requirements as constraints helps distinguish a clever implementation from one that actually meets the project's obligations.",
  "A controlled experiment tries to isolate the effect of a factor by varying it while holding relevant other conditions stable. To compare two caching policies, use comparable workloads and measure the same outcomes. If the workload changes at the same time, a faster result may not be caused by the policy. Real experiments often need repetition and careful design to handle variation. The principle is to make the causal comparison credible rather than merely observe two different runs.",
  "A design review should preserve why one alternative was chosen. If a prototype showed that a proposed integration misses the required latency, record the conditions and result alongside the decision to use another approach. Future reviewers can then evaluate whether the evidence still applies. A conclusion without its support becomes difficult to challenge or update responsibly. Include uncertainties and accepted costs as well as favorable findings so the record explains the decision honestly.",
  "An interface is an agreement about both sides of an interaction. A caller may be required to supply a valid identifier, while the implementation promises a record or a defined not-found result. Specify relevant effects and failures so each side can reason about the other. If obligations are unclear, both can appear locally correct while their interaction fails. A method signature supplies part of the contract, but meaning and behavioral guarantees often need additional explanation.",
  "The expression multiplies likelihood by impact to produce a priority score. It is a simple way to combine how often a problem might occur with how costly it could be. Its usefulness depends on how those inputs are defined and estimated. Multiplying arbitrary category labels does not create a precise expected loss, and equal scores can conceal very different risks. Use the table to support discussion and prioritization while keeping the underlying scenarios and uncertainty visible."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-practicum/18");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
